      // The platform itself, which is not a gym.
      //
      // The Super Admin has to belong to a company - a FitCore account always does, and that
      // is what the tenant resolver turns into a database - but they must not be a tenant
      // employee, so their company is the platform rather than somebody's gym.
      //
      // It is registered against the master database, because that is where the Super Admin's
      // own data actually lives: the companies, the subscriptions, the platform accounts and
      // the platform audit trail. There is no separate FitCore business database and there
      // should not be one. When the Super Admin needs to look inside a tenant they name it
      // explicitly and the ordinary tenant-resolution path serves them that tenant's own
      // database - their operational records are never copied here.
      //
      // The tier is Medium because the tier ceiling applies to the Super Admin exactly as it
      // applies to everybody else. On a smaller plan their own administration screens would be
      // withheld from them, which is a correct rule producing a nonsensical result.
      {
        "CompanyCode": "FITCORE",
        "CompanyName": "FitCore Platform",
        "Tier": "Medium",
